# CHAPTER 5. WEATHERBY

Friday morning at 8 AM, Horace Weatherby found his hotel in complete chaos. Silas Carson, the night manager, met him at his office door.  “Sir, I'm so sorry. There was nothing I could do.  They simply showed up at 5 AM and started unloading everything right there in the middle of the lobby.”

“Carson! Slow down!” Weatherby snapped. “What have we talked about.  Take big problems and break them down into smaller components, and organize them.”

“Sorry, sir,” Carson bowed deferentially and took a deep breath. “Angry Mothers. Argumentative Maestro. Ballroom. Band. Castration. Caterers. Coffee. Debutante. Decorations. Donuts.”  He paused, considering what he'd left out. “Éclairs.”

“Okay, stop at ‘E’!” Weatherby ran through the list in his head.  “Wait … what about the coffee?”

“We're down to decaf, sir.”

“Dammit! Get Bellamy down here.”

Carson continued to bow as he backed out of the manager's office, only to be replaced by an imposing figure in a grey pinstripe suit and derby.

“Ah, Mr. Pierce, good morning,” Weatherby said without looking up. “I trust you got my memo from yesterday.”

“One of the little brats’ mothers hired a private dick who’s been harassing employees and guests. Yes, I read it, Mr. Weatherby.”

“Good, now I don't need to tell you how bad it makes us look when our own patrons don't trust our discretion and ability to settle in-house matters ourselves. We need to discourage this kind of behavior.”

“Yes, Mr. Weatherby.”

“But we need to do it discreetly this time.”

“Discreetly, Mr. Weatherby.”

“No, you do understand what ‘discreetly' means, don’t you?  I had the impression you were confused by the word last time.”

“By any means necessary,” the big man nodded enthusiastically.

“No, it means ‘quietly,’ ‘with secrecy,’ possibly even, if I dared hope, ‘without any of our guests knowing it even happened.’”

“Oh, okay.   I can do that, Mr. Weatherby.”

“Are you quite certain,  Mr.  Pierce?  Because I've seen no indication of this in your performance up to this point. When you found that stray rat in the storage room, you did publicly interrogate it.”

“Yes, that was wrong.  You were right to explain that to me at the time, Mr. Weatherby.”

“So, you won't be tying rats to chairs until they reveal the names of their accomplices?”

“I will not, Mr. Weatherby.”

“And certainly not in the lobby in front of the other guests.  There really was very little chance one was secretly colluding, so you did frighten more people than you intimidated.”

“Definitely not in the lobby, Mr. Weatherby.”

“Good.  Then that will be all, Mr.  Pierce.”

“Thank you, Mr. Weatherby.”

Pierce exited the room, only to be replaced by Carson a few seconds later. “Mr. Weatherby, I have Maestro Bellamy here to see you.”

“Very good, Carson, send  him in.”

Carson took three steps backward and practically shoved Bellamy through the doorway.

“Mr. Weatherby, I really must protest. These interruptions to our rehearsal time are most intolerable.  I am an artist! I must be allowed to work!”

Weatherby buried his face in his hands. “You're an artist.  The cake decorator is an artist.  The little old lady who designed the crepe paper flowers is an artist. The servers who fold the napkins into swans are artists. The florist who arranged all the centerpieces for the tables is an artist.  The bartender who puts the fruit on the toothpicks and sets the little umbrellas in the drinks is an artist.”  He grew more and more unhinged the longer he spoke.  “I am up to my elbows in artists. Do you know the difference between you and the cake decorator, the little old lady who designed the crepe paper flowers, the servers who fold the napkins into swans, the florist who arranges the centerpieces for the tables and the bartender who puts the fruit on the toothpicks and sets the little umbrellas in the drinks, Bellamy?”

Bellamy had to admit he did not.

“The difference, sir, is that you have had access to the ballroom for thirty-six hours, and the others, as of yet, have not.”

“Mr. Weatherby, the union itself has contracted us to play a gala ball tomorrow night for some very influential families. I have assembled an orchestra, researched and selected music, and am currently exploring the acoustic possibilities of a ballroom designed, in only the roughest, most general terms, to accommodate an orchestra of this size and composition. The contract specified that I will have access to said ballroom for 72 hours before the event to maximize the acoustic potential of--"

“Bellamy! The contract does not specify ‘exclusive' access.”

“It is implied.  Surely you understand that the presence of additional bodies moving throughout the room, along with the din of conversation and other gear being moved about, seriously undermines my ability to properly assess room acoustics.  My professional credibility is on the line, Mr. Weatherby. My very career hinges upon successful completion of the contract I signed.”

“Bellamy.” Weatherby placed both hands firmly on the desk in front of him, which suddenly made Bellamy glad there wasn't a pointy letter opener in sight. “You know the type of people you will be playing for, correct?”

“Of course, Mr. Weatherby.  Twenty-six of the wealthiest, most influential families in the city.”

“That is correct.  So, you understand the power they wield and the kind of things they are capable of, don't you, Mr. Bellamy?”

“I do, Mr. Weatherby.”

“Well, then understand, those people's wives are currently in my lobby, abusing the catering staff, the bartenders, and the little old ladies who make crepe paper flowers while waiting for access to the ballroom to begin decorating it for the ball. Not to mention, Mr. Bellamy, that they have drunk all the coffee and we are down to decaf.”

Bellamy scrunched his face up. “I understand, Mr. Weatherby.”

“Now, of course, I would never ask you to break a contracted arrangement on behalf of several of my paying customers, even if they are much more important than you. And I would never expect you to undermine your fine skills in acoustic architecture or compromise the quality of your performance. However, if you do not work out some sort of compromise in the next 30 minutes, I am going to use every power at my disposal to make sure the only gigs you and your orchestra ever play again involve cotton candy, the pier, and unattended children with very sticky fingers.  Are we clear, Maestro?”

“I will see what I can do, Mr. Weatherby,” Bellamy said as he quickly backed out of the room and tore across the lobby, nearly colliding with the kid and me.

“The Maestro’s really worked up about something this morning,” Reggie said.

“He’s not the problem kid.  The problem is: how does a jewel thief get the goods out of Weatherby’s office, through the lobby, and out the door without raising suspicion?”

“He makes a clandestine handoff to someone in the lobby, then leads the staff on a wild goose chase?” The kid looks hopeful, probably dreaming he’s a natural at this kind of work.

“Only in the movies, kid.  In real life, as soon as he does that, his friend points the original thief out to the doorman on his way out.  The thief gets arrested, and he never sees his ‘friend’ or the jewels again.  How many street exits does the hotel have?”

“Six: main doors, valet exit, parking garage, two fire exits, and the loading dock.”

“The main door has a doorman; the valet exit, staff; the parking garage , witnesses; the fire exits, alarms. Who monitors the loading dock?”

“Whoever is using it.  Porters, cleaning staff, cooking staff, the office managers. If there’s not a load coming in or going out, then it's deserted.”

“But always open?”

“Yeah.  I’ve seen band members sneaking away to go down there, down there, take smoke breaks.”

“You sure they’re band members?”

“Yeah, they usually take their instruments with them.  One of the trumpet players lost his mute down there yesterday.  I had to retrieve it from lost and found for him.”

“Let’s take a look at those docks, kid.”

Reggie took me down a service hallway, past an old freight elevator to the loading dock behind the hotel.  Sure enough, it was just a short distance from the kitchen, the laundry room, and the main hallway.  Close to everything, but carefully tucked out of sight so that nobody would think twice about it.  Best of all, it was completely dark, aside from a strange blue-green glow.

“What is that?” Reggie asked.

I knelt down and examined the puddle, careful not to touch anything.  “Some kind of industrial runoff,” I said.  We traced the thin stream from the puddle back around the corner of the dock to a leaky pipe sticking out of the ground at the base of the building.  Whatever it was glowed much brighter back here at the source.  “Where does this pipe come from?  What’s under this part of the hotel?”

Reggie thought for a moment.  “There’s a basement.  It’s mostly just used for junk storage.  Old, outdated furniture, art, furnishings like that.  Oh, and the boiler is down there.  I can show you where—”

“No!”

“Are you sure?”

I jerked a thumb at the glowing blue goo.  “Positive.”

“So, wait a minute.  You’re a big tough detective with a gun who takes on underworld criminals, but you’re afraid of basements?”

“I’m not afraid of basements, kid.  Don’t start spreadin’ stories like that.”

“Okay, sorry, Mr.Chase.”

“It’s creepy basements I have a problem with, and yours is definitely creepy.”

“Mr. Chase, I’ve been down there.  It’s not that creepy.”

“Oh, yeah?  See those windows?”  I gestured to a row of semi-circular windows about nine inches tall at ground level.

“Yeah.”

“They have bars on them.”

“That’s to keep people out.”

“Or is it to keep someone in?”

“I don’t think—”

“Tell me, does it have that one bulb over the staircase that mysteriously goes out when you pass under it?  Is the handrail just a little bit loose?  Is the third step from the bottom about to collapse under the weight of someone about my weight?”

“Well, kinda—”

“Does it have a smell?  No, the usual dusty, musty smell of a basement, but one of a dead former manager someone forgot to bury?”

“I am almost certain—”

“Does it have noises?  Not just the furnace or the boiler making gurgling and hissing noises, but more like a former manager moaning and screaming because his corpse has been laid out in the middle of a giant pagan resurrection symbol made up of old Christmas ornaments and broken lightbulbs?”

“Pagans don’t actually—”

“Forget it, kid.  There are two kinds of detectives in this world.  Those who wander into creepy basements, and those who live long enough to write their memoirs, and I intend to be in the second camp.”
